@SP

Your puzzle was great and a tougher Sunday than we usually get here. 

But of course I did succeed where a less fantastic man would fail. (I know you'll get the reference. I hope.)

@SP

Nah, I can't do it. I took me 50 minutes to fill about 50% of the grid and that was it. There doesn't seem to be a "reveal word" option on that website, and I can't be ar§ed to manually look up all the trivia that you annoyingly saturated the puzzle with. I got what was going on with the circles, or so I think, but both parts of the vealer are stumping me. I appreciated some of the misdirection in your clues, but that's the only positive thing I have to say. Of course, it might have been the best puzzle in the world, and I just wasn't good enough to do it (and since I don't enjoy failing, I didn't like it).

At least it wasn't full of groaners, as I had feared it might be (or the groaners are the clues/entries I didn't get...)

@SP

Why sorry? You don't have a duty to please me 🤣

I think I would have been able to do the puzzle had it not been for the trivia. Hard clues plus arcana is more than I can handle.

@Merrick

I very much agree with B.  You have forever escaped the dread "I finally got my first Friday, but everyone said it was easy" syndrome.

Congratulations!

@B thanks for the kind words. I think I had unnecessarily built them up to be something way too difficult to even contemplate for longer than I should have. I started on them a few months ago after I crossed the 1,500 puzzles completed threshold (mostly M, Tu, W, and Su)…crazy to think I waited that long!

As everyone here knows, some days things just fall into place easier for no real reason. However, I will say that more often I have found myself absolutely struggling through a Friday or Saturday, and the top comments say “Friday’s are getting too easy!” or the like. Too funny. 

I’ll take this one and run, and I’ll drop the BOOSTERSEATS here moving forward.

@john ezra

Ignorance is bliss is a sorry contraction of the original “Where ignorance is bliss, ’tis folly to be wise” (Thomas Gray, Ode on a Distant Prospect of Eton College, 1742).

The bastardization of the original is sad—because the original was so clever.

@Matt

